    <mytitle|What is constructive QFT?>

    \;

    <\itemize>
      <item><strong|Goal:> construct, with full rigour, <em|interacting>
      relativistic quantum fields, i.e. models satisfying axioms encoding
      quantum mechanics, special relativity and causality.

      <item><strong|Status:>

      <\itemize>
        <item><math|d=2>: <math|P<around|(|\<varphi\>|)><rsub|2>>,
        Yukawa<rsub|<math|2>>, sine-Gordon, ...

        <item><math|d=3>: <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>>, ...

        <item><math|d=4>: no interacting example; critical lattice
        <math|\<varphi\><rsup|4><rsub|4>> has Gaussian scaling limits
        (Aizenman\UDuminil-Copin 2021).
      </itemize>

      <item><strong|Lesson of the late 1960s\U70s:> work in <em|imaginary
      time>. A QFT becomes a probability measure on
      <math|\<cal-S\><rprime|'><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>|)>>, which looks
      like the continuum limit of a classical spin system at a critical
      point.

      <item><strong|Modern tools> (this course): stochastic quantisation,
      singular SPDEs, variational methods; RG and cluster expansions as
      complements.
    </itemize>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
