  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Where positivity of the energy is used>

    \;

    <\enumerate>
      <item><with|font-series|bold|Analyticity.>
      <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-\<mathi\>\<zeta\>*<wide|H|\<bar\>>>=<big|int>\<mathe\><rsup|-\<mathi\>\<zeta\>*\<lambda\>>\<mathd\>E<around|(|\<lambda\>|)>>
      is a contraction for <math|<math-up|Im>\<zeta\>\<leq\>0>, since
      <math|\<lambda\>\<geq\>0>: analytic in the interior, continuous up to
      the boundary.

      <item><with|font-series|bold|Uniqueness.> An analytic function on the
      half plane vanishing on <math|-\<mathi\>\<bbb-R\><rsub|+>> vanishes;
      iterate variable by variable.

      <item><with|font-series|bold|Semigroup.>
      <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-\<tau\>*<wide|H|\<bar\>>>> is a contraction
      semigroup, which can be represented as a path integral (Feynman\UKac).

      <item><with|font-series|bold|Projection onto the vacuum.>
      <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-T*<wide|H|\<bar\>>>\<to\><around|\||\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>><around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>|\|>>
      as <math|T\<to\>\<infty\>>.
    </enumerate>

    Relativistic version: Wightman functions are boundary values of functions
    analytic in the forward tube; Lorentz covariance extends them to all
    non-coincident Euclidean points (Schwinger functions are <em|symmetric>
    and <em|Euclidean invariant>).
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Discretising imaginary time>

    <\with|font-size|0.9>
      \;

      Kinetic semigroup: <math|\<mathe\><rsup|<frac|\<delta\>|2*\<varepsilon\>>*\<Delta\>><around|(|\<varphi\>,\<varphi\><rprime|'>|)>=<around|(|2*\<pi\>*\<delta\>/\<varepsilon\>|)><rsup|-N/2>*exp
      <around*|(|-<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2*\<delta\>>*<around|\||\<varphi\>-\<varphi\><rprime|'>|\|><rsup|2>|)>>.

      <with|font-series|bold|Transfer matrix> (symmetric Trotter step):

      <\equation*>
        T<rsub|\<delta\>>=\<mathe\><rsup|-\<delta\>*U/2>\<mathe\><rsup|<frac|\<delta\>|2*\<varepsilon\>>*\<Delta\>>\<mathe\><rsup|-\<delta\>*U/2>,<space|2em>T<rsub|\<delta\>>*<around|(|\<varphi\>,\<varphi\><rprime|'>|)>\<propto\>exp
        <around*|(|-<frac|\<varepsilon\>|2*\<delta\>>*<big|sum><rsub|x><around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|x>-\<varphi\><rsub|x><rprime|'>|)><rsup|2>-<frac|\<delta\>|2>*<around*|(|U<around|(|\<varphi\>|)>+U<around|(|\<varphi\><rprime|'>|)>|)>|)>,
      </equation*>

      and <math|T<rsub|\<delta\>><rsup|<around|\<lfloor\>|\<tau\>/\<delta\>|\<rfloor\>>>\<to\>\<mathe\><rsup|-\<tau\>*H<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>>
      (Trotter).

      Space-time lattice <math|\<Lambda\>=<around|(|\<delta\>*\<bbb-Z\>/T*\<bbb-Z\>|)>\<times\><around|(|\<varepsilon\>*\<bbb-Z\>/L*\<bbb-Z\>|)>>,
      <math|T=J*\<delta\>>:

      <\equation*>
        <math-up|Tr><around|(|T<rsub|\<delta\>><rsup|J>|)>\<propto\><big|int>\<mathe\><rsup|-S<rsub|\<delta\>,\<varepsilon\>><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>><big|prod><rsub|u\<in\>\<Lambda\>>\<mathd\>\<varphi\><rsub|u>,
      </equation*>

      <\equation*>
        S<rsub|\<delta\>,\<varepsilon\>><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|<around|(|\<tau\>,x|)>>\<varepsilon\>*\<delta\>*<around*|[|<tfrac|1|2><around*|(|\<partial\><rsup|\<delta\>><rsub|\<tau\>>*\<varphi\>|)><rsup|2>+<tfrac|1|2><around*|(|\<nabla\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>*\<varphi\>|)><rsup|2>+<tfrac|1|2>m<rsup|2>*\<varphi\><rsup|2>+V<around|(|\<varphi\>|)>|]>.
      </equation*>

      <strong|The Minkowski metric has become Euclidean.>
    </with>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Feynman\UKac\UNelson>

    \;

    Lattice Gibbs measure <math|\<mu\><rsup|T,L><rsub|\<delta\>,\<varepsilon\>><around|(|\<mathd\>\<varphi\>|)>=Z<rsup|-1>\<mathe\><rsup|-S<rsub|\<delta\>,\<varepsilon\>><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>><big|prod><rsub|u>\<mathd\>\<varphi\><rsub|u>>:

    <\equation*>
      <frac|<math-up|Tr><around*|(|F<rsub|1>*T<rsub|\<delta\>><rsup|k<rsub|1>>*F<rsub|2>*\<cdots\>*F<rsub|n>*T<rsub|\<delta\>><rsup|k<rsub|n>>|)>|<math-up|Tr><around|(|T<rsub|\<delta\>><rsup|J>|)>>=<big|int>F<rsub|1><around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|\<tau\><rsub|1>>|)>*\<cdots\>*F<rsub|n><around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|\<tau\><rsub|n>>|)>*<space|0.17em>\<mu\><rsup|T,L><rsub|\<delta\>,\<varepsilon\>><around|(|\<mathd\>\<varphi\>|)>.
    </equation*>

    <\proposition*>
      <\equation*>
        <around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>,F<rsub|1>\<mathe\><rsup|-<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|1>-\<tau\><rsub|2>|)>*<wide|H|\<bar\>>>F<rsub|2>*\<cdots\>*F<rsub|n>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|\<rangle\>>=lim<rsub|T\<to\>\<infty\>>
        lim<rsub|\<delta\>\<to\>0> <big|int>F<rsub|1><around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|\<tau\><rsub|1>>|)>*\<cdots\>*F<rsub|n><around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|\<tau\><rsub|n>>|)><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>\<mu\><rsup|T,L><rsub|\<delta\>,\<varepsilon\>>.
      </equation*>
    </proposition*>

    <with|font-series|bold|Sketch.> Gaussian factor <math|=> Brownian loops
    with diffusion <math|\<varepsilon\><rsup|-1>>;
    <math|\<delta\>*<big|sum><rsub|\<tau\>>U<around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|\<tau\>>|)>>
    is a Riemann sum for <math|<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|T>U<around|(|\<omega\><rsub|\<tau\>>|)>\<mathd\>\<tau\>>;
    then <math|T\<to\>\<infty\>> uses the spectral gap.

    <strong|Vacuum expectations in imaginary time are moments of a classical
    probability measure.>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Example: one oscillator and the Ornstein\UUhlenbeck process>

    \;

